{{#extend "layout" showSource=true}}

  {{#content "main"}}
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Example 3 &middot; pages</p>
    <h1>{{model.heading}}</h1>
    <p>{{model.lede}}</p>
    <p><a href="roasts/index.html">See every roast &rarr;</a></p>
  </section>

  <div class="grid">
    {{#each model.highlights}}
    <article class="card">
      <h3>{{name}}</h3>
      <p class="meta">{{note}}</p>
    </article>
    {{/each}}
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    {{> "newsletter"}}
  </div>
  {{/content}}

  {{#content "demo"}}
  <p>
    <code>.pages()</code> is <code>.page()</code> with an array. Point its model at the
    <code>models/roasts</code> folder and kiss loads every JSON file in it, then builds one
    page per entry from a single view, <code>roasts/roast.hbs</code>. The controller runs
    once per roast and hands back a slug, so the pages land at
    <code>roasts/guji-uraga.html</code> rather than <code>roast-0</code>,
    <code>roast-1</code> and so on.
  </p>
  <p>
    The listing page reuses the same data. The build callback asks
    <code>getModelByID('roasts', data)</code> for the array the fan-out already resolved and
    registers one more page with it, so the folder is read once and the index can never
    disagree with the detail pages.
  </p>
  {{/content}}

{{/extend}}
